    <section class="pi-opening" aria-labelledby="inside-introduction">
      <div class="pi-opening-inner">
        <p class="pi-kicker">INTRODUCTION / FROM THE AUTHOR</p>
        <h2 id="inside-introduction">IT WAS ROUGH<br>ON THE INSIDE.</h2>
        <div class="pi-opening-copy">
          <p>Here lies my jail journal excerpts. They reflect a journey of personal acceptance and self-love, spirituality and inner peace, radical acceptance and living with my circumstances, mental health and growing, standing on my own two legs, and overall learning about life.</p>
          <p>It was rough on the inside. I saw things I never want to see again, experienced things I never want to go through for the second time, and met people I never want to meet again. I documented it in my journals, and now I’m writing parts of it here.</p>
          <p>These journals do not tell every story about what happened around me. Most of the time I was writing about what was happening inside my head. Books were a refuge. The pages were a place to question everything, including myself. This is the journey they record.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="pi-chapter pi-pink" id="chapter-booking" aria-labelledby="chapter-booking-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker">BEFORE THE JOURNAL / BOOKING &amp; ARRIVAL</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-booking-title">BOOKING.<br>ARRIVAL.<br>GENERAL POP.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>The journal excerpts begin after I had already been inside for weeks. This is where I can tell the part that came before them: what I remember about booking, arriving, and entering general population.</p>

        <aside class="pi-author-note">
          <strong>THE WAITING ROOM / BOOKING</strong>
          <p>The only thing on the TV was an infomercial. I found it to be its own kind of punishment. There was no clock. The plastic chairs were worse than the ones at the DMV. I was in a different time zone. For someone like me, booking was a room thick with dread.</p>
          <p>All I wanted was for the process to be over so I could go lie under a blanket. I'd asked a few times at the desk how much longer it would take. The next time I asked was apparently the wrong time.</p>
          <p>"Ma'am, we're working as fast as we can. You're fucking bothering us. You need to go in the cell now."</p>
          <p>I was escorted to the holding cell. I begged for a second, then reminded myself it was futile. Still, I put my foot in the door and begged again.</p>
          <p>"Move your foot."</p>
          <p>I complied. My only company was a roll of toilet paper until another woman was escorted into the cell with me. She explained some jail tips that I immediately forgot, but she got us mats and blankets.</p>
          <p>After a while, I was taken through booking. I was fingerprinted and photographed. My personal belongings were taken. They gave me a bracelet that felt like a tattoo. <em>It's official.</em></p>
          <p>I was handed ugly red polyester clothes, brown underwear, a few scratchy blankets, and a towel the size of a large hand towel.</p>
        </aside>

        <aside class="pi-author-note">
          <strong>GETTING ADVICE / ARRIVAL</strong>
          <p>Through what seemed like a maze, a guard escorted me to where I would be held. I took steps away from my freedom and radically accepted my fate. I was here because of me—nobody else. The only thing on my mind was lying down. I should've been thinking about my safety and how to contact my family, but I was thinking about the blankets in my arms. The guard suddenly stopped at a door that looked like every other door and opened it. Gen pop. I had arrived. The guards put me in a cell and shut the door. I couldn't see the clock, which bothered me.</p>
          <figure class="pi-quote">
            <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
              <p>My first night seems so far away. I remember it so well though.</p>
              <p>It was the night of 11/22/24, and her name was Cin. Tall, maybe 5'9", and skinny, with long red hair—the kind of red that comes from a box. She told me she'd been caught with some amount [I forget] of fentanyl and heroin. For the life of me, I can't remember the specifics, but it sounded like a shit ton.</p>
              <p>I remembered seeing her in booking, so she couldn't have beaten me to the pod by much. As she arranged her things on the bottom bunk, she introduced herself and said, "Welcome to jail." I must've looked like a dumbfounded amateur. My only exposure to jail was the night I'd spent in Denver County—and, of course, TV shows.</p>
              <p>"First time in jail?" Cin asked with a friendly smile.</p>
              <p>I told her I'd been freshly sentenced and plucked right from court. I guess finding out I wasn't some basic bitch being bonded out surprised her, because she immediately launched into Jail 101. She gave me some of the most valuable advice I've ever gotten—advice that honestly goes beyond jail in some circumstances:</p>
              <p>"Only do your own time."</p>
            </blockquote>
            <figcaption>DECEMBER 31, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL ENTRY</figcaption>
          </figure>
        </aside>

        <aside class="pi-author-note">
          <strong>MORE FREEDOM? / GENERAL POPULATION</strong>
          <p>"Sucks you came in on a Thursday," Cin said, taking a bite of her apple. "Book cart days are on Wednesdays. Oh, and if you see any of this series, can you grab it for me?"</p>
          <p>We were sitting in our cell during the 22-hour lockdown, waiting for lunch. <em>Catfish</em> was on the TV across the room, where we had a good view. The sound came through a radio station we could tune in. During a commercial break, I asked Cin where to find books. I'd been in jail for one night.</p>
          <p>"People put books they're done with down on that table where the AA books are."</p>
          <p>I knew where I was going during lunch. I grabbed about five books that day.</p>
          <p>I learned a lot more about jail very quickly. There was another pod, Dorm C, where women with nonviolent charges who had behaved well could go. They could move around whenever they wanted, unlike us in Gen Pop, where we had 22-hour lockdowns.</p>
          <p>Those lockdowns were brutal. That's how I read fifteen books in twenty-three days. I wanted into Dorm C, so I quickly filled out an application.</p>
          <p>Days went by. I read more books and spent more time looking out the window, waiting for a guard to come get me and take me to Dorm C. Whenever the door opened and a guard stepped inside, I hoped they were there for me. I wanted that khaki-and-navy uniform so badly. I wanted to take classes and use the microwave whenever I wanted.</p>
          <p>One day, I was told I was moving. Until then, I'd been living without a cellie and watching women rotate in and out of my cell. I hated it. I never knew who would walk through the door. I'd heard stories about women being attacked on the toilet, having their things stolen, being watched while they slept, fighting over the light switch, and other kinds of nightmares.</p>
          <p>I was glad to be rooming with someone people said was cool, as long as I stayed on her good side and kept the room clean. <em>Easy enough?</em> I thought. I rolled up all my things, ready to walk across the pod.</p>
          <p>Erica had been in jail for a year. She had a pet spider named Gertrude, and pictures of her children and grandchildren covered her side of the cell. Like many of the other women, she was there on drug charges. Erica had a violent streak, though; she and her former best friend had gotten into a fight. She often told me about "Crack"—the name fit; the pants didn't.</p>
          <p>One day, while I was cleaning, I was told to pack my things. I was going to Dorm C. After a little more than two weeks in Gen Pop, I was done.</p>
          <p>I was glad. In Gen Pop, women with very different needs and behaviors were all housed together: women with mental illness who screamed, women who could be violent, and women who needed to be somewhere other than with nonviolent inmates. No more of that. More freedom in jail. What a paradox.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-01" aria-labelledby="chapter-01-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>01</span> DECEMBER 2024</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-01-title">A SMALL LIBRARY IN A PLACE I DIDN’T WANT TO BE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>On December 14, I'd already been inside for weeks. My entry starts with group cleaning, a deputy working the evening shift, and the new book I was reading. That was often how it worked: the day's routine would show up on the page, and then my mind would go somewhere else entirely.</p>
<p>The bunk was crowded with books. Reading didn't make the jail disappear, but for a little while I could be somewhere other than Boulder County.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>My bunk is a small library — I've got eleven books in a cache. Books are my escape. Sometimes, when I'm deep in an author's narrative, I forget I'm even here.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>DECEMBER 14, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 006 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="006">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Wrapped up in books is how I want to be known here. So far, so good. I'm known as a reader to a couple of the women. I think tomorrow I'm going to read &amp; drink tea all day. Let's try to finish one in 24 hours.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>DECEMBER 15, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 015 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="015">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>For now, I can control [sort of] what I eat, what radio stations I listen to, and what books I read. Apart from when I shower and which stall I piss in, otherwise, it's not up to me.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>DECEMBER 22, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-pink" id="chapter-02" aria-labelledby="chapter-02-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>02</span> DECEMBER 2024</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-02-title">THE WOMAN I THOUGHT NOBODY WOULD MISS.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Some of the earliest entries are brutal to read. I was preoccupied with who was noticing my absence, who would still speak to me, and whether there would be anyone waiting when I finally came home. I kept making predictions about being abandoned, then treating those predictions as if I already knew how the future would unfold.</p>
<p>Sometimes the words I used for myself were far crueler than anything anyone else had said to me. The journal doesn't hide that.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>So, I always end up there — I belong in the trash. I'm just someone to be thrown away &amp; discarded. I don't matter. I am completely convinced that most people wouldn't even notice if I left. Well, they won't even notice that I'm away for the winter. On the outside, I'm invisible.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>DECEMBER 14, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>But I know what's going to happen when I leave this place in a handful of months: Nobody will care when I return. I'll be finished with a tough time in life and there won't be anybody at the finish line saying, "Welcome home — you actually made it." Surely not saying, "What was it even like?" or "What did you learn?" Jail isn't a proud achievement, but I think surviving it is quite an accomplishment, isn't it?</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>DECEMBER 14, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 019 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="019">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>So, maybe I'm not forgotten?</p>
          <p>I have these thoughts — I'm maybe not truly alone — and consider them. It gives me pause. It contradicts how I've felt all day.</p>
          <p>Even with the girls here — I'm starting to feel less alone sometimes.</p>
          <p>How do I deal with that conflict in my narrative?</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>DECEMBER 28, 2024 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Those weren't two neat, separate problems. The fear that I had been forgotten and the conviction that I didn't deserve to be remembered fed each other. It would take much longer than a few pages to begin questioning either of them.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-03" aria-labelledby="chapter-03-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>03</span> JANUARY 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-03-title">LEARNING TO LOOK AT MYSELF DIFFERENTLY.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Books started giving me questions that weren't just variations of <em>what is wrong with me?</em> I was reading about shame and self-compassion. In January, I noticed an ugly double standard: I could be compassionate toward someone else's illness or addiction, while treating my own as evidence of a personal failure.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 030 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="030">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I mean by that — a few things: actively practicing the whole self-compassion thing, not caring what other people think, and living as a better version of myself, or just authentically — quiet, alone, and merely content.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>JANUARY 9, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>If I were literally anybody else — not me — I'd have compassion, reflexively. Just like how I think that alcoholism is a disease for others, but it's a character flaw &amp; moral failing for me personally. I'm not unique, and my problems exist in millions of others — but why can't I just recognize that and see I'm not standing all by myself? It makes no sense to me.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>JANUARY 9, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 051 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="051">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I love these types of mornings: Crossword? Complete. Almost everyone? Asleep. Noise? None. Newspaper? Read. Coffee? Hot. ♡ It's absolute bliss and I try to savor these little moments as much as I can, because Lord knows when the chaos will inevitably set in.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>JANUARY 21, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I still feel like I don't exist, but I think that's just a part of being in jail — losing one's self-identity, losing one's sense of self altogether I think. When I'm out, I just hope that my identity returns. That my sense of belonging returns, because I am afraid I have been truly abandoned by those I thought would've been there for me.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>JANUARY 25, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 063 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="063">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>So I had this brilliant suggestion: write a letter to my future self. So I did just that this morning: I wrote myself a letter to read when I get out. Just talking about some things I'm learning in jail. Or I hope to learn. Like not sweating the small, temporal stuff, appreciating things, and being a lot more optimistic.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>JANUARY 25, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 069 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="069">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>In any case, it's nice and quiet now, as everyone is still asleep. This truly is my favorite time of day. The only noise is the guard doing his check every thirty minutes. Otherwise it's completely quiet. And there is some peace in that. Enjoy it.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>JANUARY 26, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>That insight didn't magically cure my loneliness. By the end of January I was still afraid of returning to a town where I imagined I'd been forgotten. But I was also trying, sometimes deliberately, to build a relationship with myself instead of waiting for somebody else to tell me I mattered.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-purple" id="chapter-04" aria-labelledby="chapter-04-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>04</span> FEBRUARY 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-04-title">BOOKS, QUESTIONS, AND A WAY TO SIT WITH MY OWN HEAD.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>I read constantly, moved between novels and books on recovery, and wrote my way through exercises on shame, mindfulness, and self-compassion. I also met with the jail chaplain and began asking serious questions about religion and spirituality. The reading opened up a different kind of escape: not just leaving the place mentally, but learning how to stay present without getting consumed by it.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 110 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="110">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I read all day today. It was bliss. I read Jane Eyre. I love that book. Also managed to finish the awful Faerie series. Unfortunately didn't get in any nonfiction, but tomorrow is another day of nothing, so I'll read then.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>FEBRUARY 3, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 110 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="110">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>So, instead — to work against it — I'm trying to mentally envision my friends &amp; neighbors being happy to see me when I finally go home. Instead of hating me, they just wave. There's no delusions like someone will throw me parties or anything crazy — just a friendly wave.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>FEBRUARY 3, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 128 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="128">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I keep thinking of home; how much walking I'll do; how much music I'll be listening to; how much I miss it... The crystal clear water in the creek, so clean &amp; fresh. The wind dancing on the pine boughs, the smell of lodgepole &amp; ponderosa, grass, and fresh breeze.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>FEBRUARY 8, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Whoa — Chaplain is awesome! We talked for an hour and a half about religion, Christianity, sobriety, and spirituality. He's going to get me some Hindu texts &amp; the Dhammapada! And we're going to meet next week.</p>
          <p>I wonder where this spiritual journey will take me! I even grabbed a Bible (a good one) to study on my own time.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>FEBRUARY 17, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>I was reading the <em>Dhammapada</em>, exploring Hindu texts, and working through <em>Recovery Dharma</em>. Some days the dorm was so loud I wanted everyone to leave me alone. Other days, I was buried in books because I was genuinely curious what else I might learn.</p>
        <aside class="pi-author-note">
          <strong>FORGETTING THINGS / INSIDE THE JAIL</strong>
          <p>There was a constant cycle of women through the dorm. The ones that got in right away were frequent flyers. One girl came through three times in a month. She was a chronic shoplifter. I remember she used to lay on the floor of the shower and be in there for hours. A core group of us remained for just about the same amount of time, with two there for a longer amount of time with cases pending.</p>
          <p>We were generally kind to one another, understanding that we are all in shitty situations. One time, a new girl stole a lollipop off of my bed, which is something you just don't do in jail. The guards caught her on camera, and she was dealt with. There was gossip, typical drama among women, and fighting, but nothing too serious. Nothing like how unsafe gen pop could be. We never had a drug problem either except once when some girl from Gen Pop brought drugs, and then we were all strip searched. It was incredibly humiliating.</p>
          <p>All the while, I watched the minute hand on the clock tick by, wondering what everybody was up to on the outside. I wondered what a tree looked like, how the wind felt. I went for ninety-three days without going outside. The women and the beans I was given were all I had. I wondered what good food was. What a dog was. What a hug was. What life was.</p>
        </aside>

        

        
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-yellow" id="chapter-05" aria-labelledby="chapter-05-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>05</span> FEBRUARY 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-05-title">MISSING THE WORLD OUTSIDE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Jamestown kept turning up in the journal. The creek, the pines, the mountains, my dog, the familiar streets—ordinary parts of life that felt very far away. In jail, I was surrounded by people and could still feel painfully alone. At home, I pictured solitude differently: chosen, familiar, and mine.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>In this moment, I am very homesick. I want to feel the grass on my bare feet, smell the pines, hear the creek, and let the breeze kiss me. I miss my friends — my neighbors — and sitting outside. In this moment, I'd do anything to be taken back.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>FEBRUARY 25, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>Missing home wasn't just about wanting out of jail. I missed the ordinary things I'd barely thought about before: grass under my feet, the sound of running water, and the freedom to decide where to go.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 238 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="238">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Instead I will think of how we went outside today for recreation time. I was overjoyed to be in the sunshine. I shot hoops &amp; walked with Carleen.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>FEBRUARY 28, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-06" aria-labelledby="chapter-06-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>06</span> MARCH 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-06-title">A LITTLE PEACE. NOT A PERFECT TRANSFORMATION.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Early March brought changes inside the dorm, too. The women I had come to care about were beginning to leave, and I was becoming aware of how much those friendships mattered.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 249 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="249">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>She's the first of my good friends to leave. Next will be Carleen (most likely), then Michelle. Then me.
I guess that is just an indicator that life's changes are on the horizon.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>MARCH 4, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>Then, on March 13, something changed for a day. I wrote about feeling peaceful even with the noise, tension, and other people's moods around me. I wasn't claiming jail had become pleasant. I was finding out that I didn't have to react to every part of it.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>That's what the goal for the day is: don't let my vibe get killed. It's not like I feel okay with being in jail. But I am just able to do it and not let it bother the hell out of me. For one, I'm sick of reacting to jail. For two, I want inner peace &amp; if I can have it in jail, I think I have a good chance to maintain it on the outside.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>MARCH 13, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Something I should write is that I "ran" for a mentor position (1 of 2). Originally, I did it so that this one girl wouldn't get it, but now I'm not sure. 2 people told me that they voted for me and I have a definite 5 more votes... I never really expected to get it. Or even have people vote for me. I'm almost petrified to do it... but if my peers have confidence in me, I should too.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>MARCH 13, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>There were people inside I cared about, too. When women voted for me to be a mentor, I was surprised that they had confidence in me. Their faith in me clashed with the version of myself I had been writing about since December.</p>
<p>That feeling of peace did not last uninterrupted. Later entries still contain anger, loneliness, and thoughts about being unwanted. The progress was real, and so were the setbacks.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 269 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="269">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I finally finished the 67 page Gita intro. Next is the chapter intro and then I can finally get into it. I wonder what wisdom is in the Gita. Chaplain said it's one of his favorite books. My goal is to read half (at least) today.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>MARCH 15, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 284 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="284">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>And just like that, everything is fine again. Really don't know what the problem was. Was just so sad for three full days.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>MARCH 25, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-purple" id="chapter-07" aria-labelledby="chapter-07-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>07</span> MARCH 26, 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-07-title">THE WORLD I COULD STILL PICTURE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>Near the end of March, my mind kept returning to a particular place: Bluejay Mine and the old four-wheel-drive roads in the mountains. The descriptions of home became almost physical. I could picture the trees and hear the wind, even though I was nowhere near them.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I can almost perfectly see the forest if I close my eyes: the wind singing in the pines, the sun shining on the old 4x4 road, birds singing, not a soul around...
It's so far from this cinderblock compound with its harsh fluorescent lights &amp; sterile appearance.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>MARCH 26, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>That was the distance between the place I was stuck and the place I wanted to go. I was also making practical plans about work, transportation, and what living sober would mean after release.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 295 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="295">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I'm too busy to relapse anyway—I want a car and license. I can't get those things if I'm in jail or drunk. I need a job, therapy, and a routine.
Ah, a routine that I create—I'm so excited! I think I'm going to work with my therapists to create a routine for the week. I'll be sure to bring that to the PO. And I'll be sure to hit the ground running with looking for a job.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>MARCH 29, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-pink" id="chapter-08" aria-labelledby="chapter-08-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>08</span> APRIL 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-08-title">SOMETHING CAME AWAKE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>In the last weeks I was counting days and rehearsing life after release: work, therapy, sobriety, books, walking, and being home with Banjo. Then on April 4 I wrote about an unfamiliar feeling. I was starting to wonder whether I could see myself as something other than damaged or disposable.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>The feeling—this strange emotion—persists. I feel fresh, anew, &amp; like there is nothing that can touch me. Like I was blind &amp; can see everything so clearly. Like I didn't know, couldn't know better before. But over these past 4 1/2 months with almost nothing, I've come through. I was right when I told myself that the only way out is through. And I was right. I was abandoned, I was treated as subhuman, I grew up, I became social again. Something long asleep came awake inside of me.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 4, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>I hadn't become somebody else overnight. The journal still held the same questions and worries. But this entry sounds different from December. It says I had been hurt, had lived through it, and was beginning to see that surviving wasn't the same thing as being worthless.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 323 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="323">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Oh man, have I gotten good at chit-chatting. I can't wait to chew the fat with the old guys, hear jokes, and be a new &amp; social version of Mel. I also can't wait for that!</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>APRIL 7, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-yellow" id="chapter-09" aria-labelledby="chapter-09-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>09</span> APRIL 10–11, 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-09-title">THE PART WHERE I STILL HAD TO CHOOSE.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>I didn't leave jail with a perfect mindset or a guarantee about the future. In the final days, I was still worried about returning to drinking and slipping back into old patterns. Therapy, mindfulness, and radical acceptance were things I had practiced, not boxes I could check off forever.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 330 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="330">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>It seems so incredibly simple from my point of view in jail: just stay sober. It can't be that hard, can it? I think. Apparently it was that hard, otherwise I wouldn't be sitting here right now.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>APRIL 9, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I got good advice during therapy. Basically be mindful and just acknowledge the passing of impermanent thoughts.
The alcoholic &amp; bipolar sides will unfortunately have eternal voices for the rest of my life. I just have to accept that, but I also have the choice to act on what they say. I can't ignore it, just have to let it go. I've been getting plenty of practice in jail...
Practicing radical acceptance is also something I've had a lot of practice with in jail. I never really wrestled with the sentence from the beginning.
So there it is.</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 10, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>I truly am wanting to start my life again. I came to jail just broken and dead inside. Somehow my passion for living came back. I'm trying to be an optimist now. I have so much hope, despite wanting to get rid of it earlier. I am hopeful for my future &amp; what it holds. I can't wait to see what's coming!</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 11, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>On April 11, I wrote about wanting my family to be proud and wanting to stay sober. The same pages also hold an enormous amount of hope. I hadn't forgotten why I was there; I was trying to decide how to live when I got out.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="pi-chapter pi-dark" id="chapter-10" aria-labelledby="chapter-10-title">
      <div class="pi-chapter-heading">
        <p class="pi-kicker"><span>10</span> APRIL 13, 2025</p>
        <h2 id="chapter-10-title">THE DAY THE COUNTDOWN ENDED.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="pi-story">
        <p>On April 12, the waiting had narrowed down to a single day. Even as I packed my things, I kept thinking about what the outside would feel like.</p>

        <!-- JOURNAL SOURCE: PHOTO 342 -->
        <figure class="pi-quote" data-journal-photo="342">
          <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>Oooh, I can't wait! I am super excited to feel the fresh air on my skin. And feel the breeze and sun on me. Then I can start the process of reacquainting myself with what my everyday life will look like. I just wish I could fast forward to tomorrow.</p>
          </blockquote>
          <figcaption>APRIL 12, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>On the last morning recorded in the journal, I was nervous and excited. I wrote about waiting for lockdown to end, drinking coffee with Carleen, and the farewell cake the women had made me the night before. I knew I would miss her. After months of writing about being forgotten, I was leaving with people I wanted to remember.</p>
      <figure class="pi-quote">
        <blockquote cite="#pi-source-note">
          <p>It doesn't feel real at all. Like it'll just be another day in jail. OR there's some big mistake and I'll be stuck here. OR I'll have to wait hours for all of this to be over. When in 2 hours it'll most likely be over.
It still doesn't feel real.
Am I dreaming?</p>
        </blockquote>
        <figcaption>APRIL 13, 2025 <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> JOURNAL</figcaption>
      </figure>
        <p>That's where the journal ends: before the actual moment I walked out, while I was still wondering if it could really be happening. I don't want to invent the rest of that morning just to give this story a tidy ending. The last question I wrote is enough—for now.</p>
        <aside class="pi-author-note">
          <strong>ON THE OUTSIDE / RELEASE DAY</strong>
          <p>“Thank you for treating us like humans,” I told Gwyn as he walked me to Booking.</p>
          <p>“I just treat you all with respect,” he replied without looking at me. I knew he cared about his job and about keeping us safe.</p>
          <p>“Good luck,” he said. He opened the door, let me walk in, and then shut it behind me.</p>
          <p>Luckily, my clothes still fit, even though I knew I was much bigger going out than when I went in. I got my things back, and they snipped off the bracelet.</p>
          <p>“Do you want to keep this?” the guard asked.</p>
          <p>“No, thank you,” I said simply.</p>
          <p>“Do you need a bus pass?” she asked.</p>
          <p>“No, I have a ride.”</p>
          <p>I was directed to the exit. On my way out, I looked at the same plastic chairs I’d sat in five months earlier.</p>
          <p>I opened the door, and the vastness of the scenery overwhelmed me. I stared at the Flatirons. They were majestic and massive. The cars on the road flowed so freely, as if they had no rules to obey. The parking lot was enormous. The wind felt good on my skin.</p>
          <p>Regular clothes felt strange. Regular shoes felt strange after wearing shoes made from some Croc-material-gone-wrong.</p>
          <p>I searched the parking lot and saw my parents’ car. They were getting out as I started walking over. We hugged, and it felt good to hug people again—especially people close to me. I’d hugged the girls in jail, but it wasn’t the same.</p>
          <p>We got in the car and headed to the grocery store to restock my refrigerator. Then we drove up the mountain to Jamestown—to my apartment, my dog, and my life.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>

